<item>
            <title>[LU-20403] Refresh import conn on primary migration</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20403</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;ptlrpc_connection_get() resolves the peer primary once via LNetPrimaryNID() and freezes it in the cached connection. Reconnect reuses that connection and never re-resolves, and ptl_send_rpc() keys the reply ME on the frozen NID. When LNet&apos;s primary for the peer later migrates, the reply ME stops matching the source NID LNet stamps on the reply, because lnet_parse() normalizes that source to the current primary. This happens for any peer whose primary we don&apos;t lock, i.e. every client peer as seen from a server. Once we&apos;re in that state every reply takes the expensive lnet_try_match_md() same-peer fallback (two lnet_find_peer() lookups under lnet_net_lock plus a CWARN), and if the peer table is corrupt the mismatch becomes an outright drop. On a reverse-channel AST a dropped reply means the server evicts the client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add LNetPeerPrimaryNID(), a non-blocking primary lookup that neither runs discovery nor allocates a peer, and call it from ptl_send_rpc(). On a detected migration, get a connection keyed on the current primary and swap imp_connection before the reply ME is posted. ptl_send_rpc() is the chokepoint both client RPCs and server reverse-channel ASTs pass through, so the one hook covers both directions, and it self-heals after a reconnect because the next send re-checks.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
